      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the role of an ultra-low-dose dual-source CT coronary angiography (CTCA) scan with high pitch for delimiting the range of the subsequent standard CTCA scan."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "30 patients with an indication for CTCA were prospectively examined using a two-scan dual-source CTCA protocol (2.0\u2009×\u200964.0\u2009×\u20090.6\u2009mm; pitch, 3.4; rotation time of 280\u2009ms; 100\u2009kV): Scan 1 was acquired with one-fifth of the tube current suggested by the automatic exposure control software [CareDose 4D™ (Siemens Healthcare, Erlangen, Germany) using 100\u2009kV and 370\u2009mAs as a reference] with the scan length from the tracheal bifurcation to the diaphragmatic border. Scan 2 was acquired with standard tube current extending with reduced scan length based on Scan 1. Nine central coronary artery segments were analysed qualitatively on both scans."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Scan 2 (105.1\u2009±\u200910.1\u2009mm) was significantly shorter than Scan 1 (127.0\u2009±\u20098.7\u2009mm). Image quality scores were significantly better for Scan 2. However, in 5 of 6 (83%) patients with stenotic coronary artery disease, a stenosis was already detected in Scan 1 and in 13 of 24 (54%) patients with non-stenotic coronary arteries, a stenosis was already excluded by Scan 1. Using Scan 2 as reference, the positive- and negative-predictive value of Scan 1 was 83% (5 of 6 patients) and 100% (13 of 13 patients), respectively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "An ultra-low-dose CTCA planning scan enables a reliable scan length reduction of the following standard CTCA scan and allows for correct diagnosis in a substantial proportion of patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "ADVANCES IN KNOWLEDGE",
          "content": "Further dose reductions are possible owing to a change in the individual patient's imaging strategy as a prior ultra-low-dose CTCA scan may already rule out the presence of a stenosis or may lead to a direct transferal to an invasive catheter procedure."
        }
      ]},
